<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#prahok">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prahok</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as prâhŏk, Cambodian cheese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prahok is a salted and fermented fish paste, usually of mudfish, used in Cambodian cuisine as a seasoning or condiment. It began as a way of preserving fish for the part of the year when fresh fish was scarce. The fish are trampled to remove scales and entrails, salted, sun-dried and pounded, then left to ripen in open earthenware jars for about a month, the liquid drawn off each day serving as fish sauce. Its strong smell has earned it the nickname 'Cambodian cheese'. In rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> it is commonly eaten with steamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> as an important source of protein, and it flavours soups, sauces and fish amok.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prahok.jpg" alt="Prahok" width="330" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paxse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fried_Prahok_meal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
